---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../consts';
---

<PageLayout
  title="About ContextLife"
  description="ContextLife (Honest Picture) is a research-backed tool and library that helps people understand where they actually stand in life — using real data, not advice or judgment."
  path="/about"
  eyebrow="About"
  heading="A mirror made of data."
  lede="ContextLife exists to answer one quiet, near-universal question — am I okay? — not with reassurance or alarm, but with honest context."
>
  <h2>Why this exists</h2>
  <p>
    Almost everyone, at some point, measures their life against a standard and
    comes up short. The standard is usually invisible and almost always wrong: a
    blend of curated social feeds, the most successful people in your particular
    circle, and a timeline you absorbed without ever checking it against reality.
  </p>
  <p>
    You see your own life from the inside — every doubt, every gap, every
    unfinished thing. You see everyone else from the outside, at their most
    composed. The result is a feeling of being behind a pace that, in the actual
    population data, frequently does not exist.
  </p>
  <p>
    {SITE.name} takes the specific facts of your life — your age, income, savings,
    time, relationships, health, and sense of direction — and places them inside
    real research and real population data. Not to tell you that you are doing well
    or badly. To show you what your situation actually looks like from the outside.
  </p>

  <h2>What we are — and what we are not</h2>
  <p>
    We are a publication and a tool, built on publicly available research,
    government statistics, and peer-reviewed studies. We are not a personality
    quiz, a productivity app, a self-help brand, or a substitute for therapy or
    professional advice. We do not sell a transformation. We do not promise that
    seeing your situation clearly will fix it.
  </p>
  <p>
    We try to use careful, honest language throughout — "the data suggests," "most
    people in your situation report," "research on this is mixed." When evidence is
    contested or weak, we say so rather than rounding it up into a clean headline.
  </p>

  <h2>How we handle the hard parts</h2>
  <p>
    Some of what this site discusses — money stress, loneliness, a sense of wasted
    time — is genuinely painful. Our commitment is to be accurate and humane at the
    same time: to never minimise a real difficulty, and never inflate a common
    experience into a personal failing. Where someone may need real help, we say
    so plainly and point toward qualified professionals.
  </p>

  <h2>Our principles</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Trust first.</strong> If a claim is not backed by a real, citable source, it does not go on the page.</li>
    <li><strong>Context, not verdicts.</strong> We show you the distribution. We do not grade your place in it.</li>
    <li><strong>Privacy by design.</strong> The assessment runs in your browser. Your answers are never stored or transmitted.</li>
    <li><strong>Honest about uncertainty.</strong> We distinguish strong findings from weak ones, and say when research is mixed.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Get in touch</h2>
  <p>
    Questions, corrections, or a source you think we should use? We take accuracy
    seriously and genuinely want to hear about errors. Visit our
    <a href="/contact">contact page</a>, read our
    <a href="/research-methodology">research methodology</a>, or see
    <a href="/data-sources">every source we use</a>.
  </p>
</PageLayout>
